1. Callback Hell is a situation in JavaScript where multiple asynchronous callbacks are nested inside one another,
   making the code hard to read, maintain, and debug.

A good example is an e-commerce order process:

Suppose a user places an order. First, we need to create the order, then process the payment, then update the inventory, 
and finally send a confirmation email. If each operation depends on the previous one and is written using callbacks, we can 
end up with deeply nested callbacks. This is called callback hell.

placeOrder(cart, (order) => {
    processPayment(order, (payment) => {
        updateInventory(order, (inventory) => {
            sendConfirmationEmail(order, (email) => {
                console.log("Order completed");
            });
        });
    });
});

Why is this callback hell?

Because each operation depends on the result of the previous operation, so callbacks become nested inside callbacks:

placeOrder
   ↓
 processPayment
      ↓
   updateInventory
         ↓
      sendEmail

2. Inversion of Control (IoC) in JavaScript means:

You give control of when and how your function is executed to another function or system.

This is one of the important problems associated with callbacks.

Simple example
function fetchData(callback) {
    // Some asynchronous operation
    setTimeout(() => {
        callback("Data received");
    }, 2000);
}

fetchData((data) => {
    console.log(data);
});

Here, you don't decide when the callback runs.

You give your callback to fetchData():

(data) => {
    console.log(data);
}

And fetchData() decides:

when to call it
whether to call it
potentially how many times to call it
what data to pass to it

That's Inversion of Control.

Why is it called "inversion"?

Normally, you might think:

My code → controls → when function executes

With a callback:

My code → gives function → to another function
                         ↓
                  controls execution

The control has effectively been handed over.

